use Libraries.Web.Page.QuorumPage

/*
    Tagging And Structure In ARIA.
    Converted from the old Lesson page by plan 13's generator: the same
    words, now without Tailwind.
*/
class TaggingAndStructureInARIA is QuorumPage
    // A double quote, for code and words that hold one.
    text dq = ""

    on create
        SetLocation("tutorials/accessibility/taggingandstructureinARIA.html")
        // The name lists and links use, and the series and track it belongs
        // to: its section and picture.
        SetShortTitle("Tagging And Structure In ARIA")
        SetSectionName("Technical Accessibility")
        SetCardImage("/media/gettingStarted.png")
        // For search engines and link previews, and what lists of pages say.
        SetDescription("Learn the underlying structure in ARIA to communicate the organization and purpose of content to assistive technologies")
        SetSummary("the underlying structure in ARIA to communicate the organization and purpose of content to assistive technologies")
    end

    action Setup
        dq = dq:GetDoubleQuote()
        SetWebPageTitle("Tagging And Structure In ARIA - Quorum Programming Language")
        Title("Tagging And Structure In ARIA")
        Paragraph("ARIA relies on a clear underlying structure, using roles and tags to communicate the organization and purpose of content to assistive technologies.")
        Overview()
        WarmUp()
        Explore()
        Engage()
        WrapUp()
    end

    private action Overview
        Section("Overview")
        Paragraph("In this lesson, you will continue exploring the basics of the Accessible Rich Internet Applications (ARIA) standard, with a focus on how structure affects accessibility. Assistive technologies rely on well-structured content to support effective navigation and comprehension. Using appropriate tags and elements, such as headings, lists, landmarks, and tables, helps ensure your content is organized in a clear and meaningful way.")
        Subsection("Goals")
        Paragraph("You have the following goals for this lesson:")
        BulletList()
        ListItem("Understand how document structure supports accessibility")
        ListItem("Learn how semantic HTML can convey meaning and relationships between elements")
        ListItem("Identify common structural issues that affects screen reader or keyboard access")
        ListItem("Compare the use of semantic HTML versus ARIA for conveying structure")
    end

    private action WarmUp
        Section("Warm up")
        Paragraph("In previous lessons, you learned that structure plays a key role in how screen readers convey information. Now, consider this: if ARIA changes the meaning or role of elements on a page, how might that affect features like the Rotor on iOS tablets, Quick Nav on Mac desktops, or Browse Mode in NVDA? Think about how these navigation tools rely on meaningful structure, and what might happen if that structure is altered or unclear.")
        Subsection("Vocabulary")
        Paragraph("You will be learning about the following vocabulary words:")
        Table("Vocabulary")
        TableHeader():Cell("﻿Term"):Cell("Definition")
        TableRow():Cell("ARIA Document Structure Roles"):Cell("A category of ARIA roles that define the organization and hierarchy of content on a web page.")
        TableRow():Cell("ARIA Landmark Roles"):Cell("A category of ARIA roles that identify major regions of a page to provide orientation and navigation clues")
        TableRow():Cell("ARIA Widget Roles"):Cell("A category of ARIA roles that represent interactive components of the user interface")
        TableRow():Cell("ARIA Application Role"):Cell("An ARIA ‘widget’ role that defines parts of a page as interactive applications, changing how screen readers navigate and interact with them.")
        TableRow():Cell("ARIA Table Role"):Cell("An ARIA role that describes layout elements as a table so screen readers can convey row and column relationships. However, using the semantic HTML table is preferred.")
        TableRow():Cell("Accessibility Tree"):Cell("A browser-generated structure that shows how elements interact with assistive technology. It is built from the underlying UI elements and reflects only the parts of the interface that are relevant for accessibility.")
        TableRow():Cell("Virtual Cursor"):Cell("A concept used by screen readers related to navigation, especially navigation on the screen independently of the current keyboard focus.")
        Subsection("Web Content Accessibility Standards")
        Paragraph("This lesson covers the following "):Link("standards", "https://www.w3.org/WAI/standards-guidelines/wcag/"):Text(":")
        BulletList()
        ListItem("WCAG 2.2 - 1.1.1 Non-text Content")
        ListItem("WCAG 2.2 - 1.3.1 Info and Relationships")
        ListItem("WCAG 2.2 - 2.1.1 Keyboard")
        ListItem("WCAG 2.2 - 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value")
    end

    private action Explore
        Section("Explore")
        Paragraph("In the last lesson you were introduced to what an ARIA role is and a few types of roles you may encounter. Remember that ARIA roles describe what an element is and adds semantic meaning to it. There are two more categories of ARIA roles that can help manage accessibility. Document Structure roles convey structure and organization and Landmark roles identify high-level regions of a web page for navigation and orientation.")
        Subsection("Document Structure Roles")
        Paragraph():Strong("Document Structure roles"):Text(" give a description for a section of content. These roles are meant to show relationships and organization of content. Assistive technologies make use of these roles to give more information about the structural context of the elements. These roles might not add functionality, but they cover common usage patterns. For example, if an accessibility device comes across a toolbar, they might be familiar with toolbars and thus change the user interface, in any number of ways for any number of different kinds of people with any number of disabilities.")
        Paragraph("Here are some of the Document Structure roles that have no native HTML equivalents:")
        Table("Document Structure Roles without Semantic HTML Equivalents")
        TableHeader():Cell("﻿Role"):Cell("Description")
        TableRow():Cell("toolbar"):Cell("Container for a group of controls, usually buttons, usually visually compact and related to formatting and commands")
        TableRow():Cell("tooltip"):Cell("Descriptive message that appears when an element receives a hover or focus")
        TableRow():Cell("feed"):Cell("Container for a stream of content")
        TableRow():Cell("math"):Cell("Container for mathematical expressions (usually used with MathML)")
        TableRow():Cell("presentation/none"):Cell("Removes the semantic meaning of an element. Unlike aria-hidden this does not hide the item completely from assistive technologies")
        TableRow():Cell("note"):Cell("Represents a side comment or annotation related to nearby content")
        Paragraph("Modern HTML has a variety of tags that cover a lot of structure roles ARIA has to offer. The roles still exist and can be used but for the following roles there is an HTML equivalent that should be used instead:")
        Table("Document Structure Roles With Semantic HTML Equivalents")
        TableHeader():Cell("﻿Role"):Cell("Native HTML"):Cell("Description")
        TableRow():Cell("article"):Cell("< article >"):Cell("Represents independent, self-contained content such as a news item")
        TableRow():Cell("cell"):Cell("< td >"):Cell("A single data cell in a table")
        TableRow():Cell("columnheader"):Cell("< th scope=" + dq + "col" + dq + " >"):Cell("Header for a column of cells in a table")
        TableRow():Cell("definition"):Cell("< dfn >"):Cell("Indicates the defining instance of a term")
        TableRow():Cell("figure"):Cell("< figure >"):Cell("Represents content like an image or diagram with a caption.")
        TableRow():Cell("group"):Cell("< fieldset >"):Cell("Groups related items or controls, often used with widgets")
        TableRow():Cell("heading"):Cell("< h1 > through < h6 >"):Cell("Marks a heading level. Screen readers make use of these for structure and navigation")
        TableRow():Cell("img"):Cell("< img > or < picture >"):Cell("Represents an image. The role makes it possible to group several img tags into a single image.")
        TableRow():Cell("list"):Cell("< ul > or < ol >"):Cell("Container for list items in either ordered or unordered format")
        TableRow():Cell("listitem"):Cell("< li >"):Cell("An item in a list")
        TableRow():Cell("meter"):Cell("< meter >"):Cell("Shows a measurement within a known range (e.g. disk usage)")
        TableRow():Cell("row"):Cell("< tr >"):Cell("A row of cells in a table or grid")
        TableRow():Cell("rowgroup"):Cell("< thead >, < tfoot >, < tbody >"):Cell("Groups of rows in a table")
        TableRow():Cell("rowheader"):Cell("< th scope=" + dq + "row" + dq + " >"):Cell("Header for a row of cells")
        TableRow():Cell("separator"):Cell("< hr > (if not focusable)"):Cell("Divider between content, only needed if the separator is interactive")
        TableRow():Cell("table"):Cell("< table >"):Cell("A data table")
        TableRow():Cell("term"):Cell("< dfn >"):Cell("The term being defined in a definition list or glossary")
        Subsubsection("Headings")
        Paragraph("Headings provide a clear outline of content, allowing users to scan and understand the structure of a page. Screen readers often rely on heading levels (e.g., <h1> through <h6>) to generate an overview of the page and enable quick navigation. Proper heading hierarchy is essential, skipping levels or using headings purely for visual styling can disrupt accessibility.")
        Subsubsection("Lists")
        Paragraph("Lists group related items together, helping users interpret information more efficiently. Whether ordered (<ol>) or unordered (<ul>), lists communicate structure to assistive technologies and maintain consistency in content presentation. Avoid using visual spacing or symbols in place of semantic list elements to ensure content remains navigable and meaningful.")
        Subsubsection("Tables")
        Paragraph():Strong("Tables"):Text(" are a visualization tool and used widely across the web. Because of this, screen readers give tables extra affordances that allow users to navigate them easier and read out relevant information about an individual cell. They are mentioned here because they require a small amount of care with tagging.")
        Paragraph("The tags related to tables are examples of Document Structure roles that add meaning to cells by describing the relationships between cells and where they fit in the structure. Here is an example of a bad HTML table:")
        CodeExample("<table>
  <tr>
    <td>Product</td>
    <td>Price</td>
    <td>In Stock</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>Laptop</td>
    <td>$999</td>
    <td>Yes</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>Monitor</td>
    <td>$199</td>
    <td>No</td>
  </tr>
</table>")
        Paragraph("If rendered in the browser, although it might look fine visually with this structure, these tags could negatively impact the experience of a user that is using a page's hidden structure. It is common for the first row of a table to be the header cells that describe the cells below them. For a screen reader to provide this additional information to a user, the table needs to be marked up correctly. Here is an example of a correctly formatted HTML table:")
        CodeExample("<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th scope='col'>Product</th>
      <th scope='col'>Price</th>
      <th scope='col'>In Stock</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>Laptop</td>
      <td>$999</td>
      <td>Yes</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Monitor</td>
      <td>$199</td>
      <td>No</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>")
        Paragraph("Now this table has a proper header section with <th> elements that also describe how they are headers for a column and not a row. And now the body and header section are properly sectioned. The cells and rows now have a specified relationship. For example the properly formatted table could be read row by row by a screen reader like ‘Product: Laptop, Price: $99, In Stock: Yes.’ As a reminder, many types of accessibility tools use structure, but the choice becomes aurally obvious in a screen reader.")
        Paragraph("For tables, the native HTML elements cover enough that you should not need to use the associated ARIA roles to make a native HTML table fully accessible. Here are some of the elements you can use:")
        Table("HTML Table Elements")
        TableHeader():Cell("﻿Element"):Cell("Purpose"):Cell("Notes")
        TableRow():Cell("< table >"):Cell("Container for all the table content"):Cell("Always included as it is the main container element")
        TableRow():Cell("< caption >"):Cell("Provides a title or summary for the table"):Cell("Must be the first child of < table >")
        TableRow():Cell("< thead >"):Cell("Groups header rows at the top of the table"):Cell("Improves clarity but not optional")
        TableRow():Cell("< tbody >"):Cell("Groups body rows (main data)"):Cell("Optional but semantically helpful")
        TableRow():Cell("< tfoot >"):Cell("Groups footer rows"):Cell("Usually comes after < tbody >")
        TableRow():Cell("< tr >"):Cell("Defines a row of cells"):Cell("Used within < thead >, < tbody >, and < tfoot >")
        TableRow():Cell("< th >"):Cell("Defines a header cell (column or row header)"):Cell("Use scope=’col’ for column headers and scope=’row’ for row headers")
        TableRow():Cell("< td >"):Cell("Defines a standard data cell"):Cell("Must be used inside a < tr >")
        TableRow():Cell("scope"):Cell("Attribute that tells screen readers the direction of a header"):Cell("Possible values are row, col, rowgroup, colgroup")
        TableRow():Cell("headers"):Cell("Associates a data cell with one or more headers"):Cell("Uses id from a < th > element and used inside a < td > element")
        TableRow():Cell("colspan/rowspan"):Cell("Spans a cell across multiple columns or rows"):Cell("This can complicate screen reader navigation so try to avoid overusing cells that span multiple columns or rows")
        Subsection("Landmark Roles")
        Paragraph():Strong("Landmark roles"):Text(" are less fine grained than Document Structure roles. These roles identify a high-level region of a web page. Landmarks allow screen reader users to jump quickly to major parts of a page. A lot of these regions typically only show up once per page and these semantic regions then get exposed in the accessibility tree. The "):Strong("accessibility tree"):Text(", typically stored in the computer's operating system, is a location where all accessibility devices gather information from. How it works is complex and varies in its implementation. For web developers, it is kind of similar to working in the DOM, but usually much more low level and with roles that are mapped at the operating system level and generally less the web level.")
        Table("Landmark Roles With Semantic HTML Equivalents")
        TableHeader():Cell("﻿Role"):Cell("Native HTML"):Cell("Description")
        TableRow():Cell("banner"):Cell("< header >"):Cell("Defines a site-wide header area, usually contains branding or a logo")
        TableRow():Cell("complementary"):Cell("< aside >"):Cell("Marks content that only indirectly related to the main content, usually shown as a side bar or callout box")
        TableRow():Cell("contentinfo"):Cell("< footer >"):Cell("A site-wide footer area which usually contains identifying information like copyright information or privacy statements.")
        TableRow():Cell("form"):Cell("< form >"):Cell("Identifies a section of a document with interactive controls. Usually only exposed if the form has a label or accessible name.")
        TableRow():Cell("main"):Cell("< main >"):Cell("Used for the dominant content of the body of the document. Should only ever be used once per page")
        TableRow():Cell("navigation"):Cell("< nav >"):Cell("Identifies a group of links used for navigating through a page or website")
        TableRow():Cell("region"):Cell("< section >"):Cell("Generic standalone section of a document. Only used if a section is deemed important enough to be a landmark and no other more specific element can represent it.")
        TableRow():Cell("search"):Cell("< search >"):Cell("Identifies the search functionality of a page. Encompasses all elements related to the search on of the most common elements inside would be < input type=" + dq + "search" + dq + " >")
        Paragraph("When using landmark roles, it is important to apply them correctly to maintain clarity and consistency for assistive technology users. Remember that roles like main and banner should appear only once per page to prevent confusion, while others like navigation can appear multiple times as long as they are clearly distinguished. To help users differentiate between similar landmarks, especially when there are multiples of the same type, you can use attributes like aria-label or aria-labelledby to give each region a unique, descriptive name. For example, labeling different navigation areas as ‘Main Menu’ and ‘Footer Links.’ This ensures screen reader users can easily understand the purpose of each section and move between them efficiently.")
        Subsubsection("Main")
        Paragraph("A common landmark is main (<main>), and it identifies the primary content of a page, excluding headers, footers, and sidebars. This allows users to bypass repetitive content and focus directly on the core material. "):Strong("There should be only one main region per page"):Text(" to maintain clarity and avoid confusion.")
        Subsubsection("Navigation")
        Paragraph("Another common landmark role is navigation (typically implemented as <nav>), it identifies a section of the page containing links to other areas or pages. This helps users quickly locate site menus or other navigational aids using screen reader region shortcuts or rotor features. Each page can have multiple navigation landmarks, but they should be clearly labeled to differentiate them.")
        Subsection("Widget Roles")
        Paragraph("As a reminder from the previous lesson, widget roles describe interactive components like buttons, sliders, and dialogs. These roles provide essential information about how users can interact with elements. They are especially important when native HTML elements are not used or when behavior extends beyond standard controls.")
        Subsubsection("Application")
        Paragraph("It might be the case that you have created a web experience that is more like a desktop application than a normal web page. There could be a number of interactions and commands where navigating by headers or jumping to landmarks does not work with this application and would be harder for screen reader users to use. That is where the ARIA widget role application comes into play. This role is essential, dangerous, confusing, messy, confusing, inconsistent, and so many other things. To say the least, while it does have use and it is good to know about it, try to avoid using it unless you absolutely know what you are doing. Also, be aware that no matter how you use it, others will likely tell you that you are doing it wrong.")
        Paragraph("The "):Strong("application role"):Text(" signals to a screen reader that a region of the page should be treated differently and to not provide traditional screen reader affordances. This gives developers finer control over keyboard interactions and focus management in that region. Or at least all of that is true in theory. In practice, it never really works out and again considerable caution and ample testing is necessary if you believe you need this role.")
        Paragraph("There also exists the aria-roledescription attribute which can be added to custom widgets to change how a screen reader expresses the name of that role. Take this code example:")
        CodeExample("<div role='application' aria-roledescription='Block' aria-label='My Component'>
    My Component
</div>")
        Paragraph("Without the role description a screen reader would vocalize this as ‘application, My Component’. The word application does not really give the user much information. The role description gives you a way to override the name to something you as a developer would find more helpful. For this example it could be read as ‘Block, My Component’ and in the context of your own application could be more useful for the screen reader experience.")
        Paragraph("Because encountering an element with role='application' can be disruptive to the screen reader experience, it is a role to be used "):Emphasis("cautiously"):Text(". Screen readers lose what is known as the "):Strong("virtual cursor"):Text(" and non-interactive elements like images or paragraphs cannot be reached. Certain shortcut keys screen readers add to navigate web pages stop working so those keys are opened up to be used by your application. Because of this, unfortunately results you get using this role may vary.")
        Paragraph("An incorrectly applied or misused role='application' could be detrimental for the usability of a site for a screen reader user. So the implementation and result can vary between system and assistive technology. For example, on mobile devices, the major screen readers Talkback and VoiceOver tend to ignore the role and operate as if it was not there. As such, it is important to test on various platforms when attempting to use this role. Even though the role might not always work as expected, sometimes it is useful to try to get a screen reader to relinquish control and let the developers try to make a more accessible experience than might have not otherwise been possible. Put simply, the platforms can sometimes be a real hindrance to accessibility, even with good intentions, and thus the application role is an optional tool to use in these cases.")
    end

    private action Engage
        Section("Engage")
        Paragraph("Visual layouts can mislead you into thinking that assistive technologies can agree on its structure. For this activity you will be given a broken table that with the right styling might look fine but is missing all of the semantic structure. Your goal is to fix it.")
        Subsection("Directions")
        Paragraph("Below are some tables that might resemble a grid of data. It has no structure and only uses one type of element. Your job is to turn this into a screen reader friendly table using either the native HTML tags and ARIA properties as you see fit. For each table, do the following:")
        BulletList()
        ListItem("Imagine what it would say in your head as navigating")
        ListItem("Actually traverse the table with various technologies and check")
        ListItem("Fix the table and recheck")
        Paragraph("To do this, you can either copy paste the HTML into an HTML file and open it in a browser or copy the code and use an online HTML editor like "):Link("W3Schools TryIt Editor", "https://www.w3schools.com/html/tryit.asp?filename=tryhtml_default"):Text(".")
        Paragraph("First, take the following table code:")
        CodeExample("<div class='table'>
  <div class='caption'>Users</div>
  <div class='row'>
    <div class='cell'>Name</div>
    <div class='cell'>Email</div>
    <div class='cell'>Status</div>
  </div>
  <div class='row'>
    <div class='cell'>Jane Doe</div>
    <div class='cell'>jane@example.com</div>
    <div class='cell'>Active</div>
  </div>
  <div class='row'>
    <div class='cell'>John Smith</div>
    <div class='cell'>john@example.com</div>
    <div class='cell'>Inactive</div>
  </div>
</div>")
        Paragraph("As part of your fixes, ensure that the header cells are tagged appropriately and that each cell is properly associated with its header. Once you have finished fixing the first table, try to correct the next table. Remember that cells are sometimes associated with more than one header:")
        CodeExample("<div class='table'>
  <div class='caption'>Sales info</div>
  <div class='row header'>
    <div class='cell'></div>
    <div class='cell'>Monday</div>
    <div class='cell'>Tuesday</div>
    <div class='cell'>Wednesday</div>
  </div>
  <div class='row'>
    <div class='cell'>North</div>
    <div class='cell'>$1,200</div>
    <div class='cell'>$1,450</div>
    <div class='cell'>$1,300</div>
  </div>
  <div class='row'>
    <div class='cell'>South</div>
    <div class='cell'>$980</div>
    <div class='cell'>$1,100</div>
    <div class='cell'>$1,050</div>
  </div>
  <div class='row'>
    <div class='cell'>East</div>
    <div class='cell'>$1,500</div>
    <div class='cell'>$1,600</div>
    <div class='cell'>$1,550</div>
  </div>
</div>")
    end

    private action WrapUp
        Section("Wrap up")
        Paragraph("ARIA is a powerful enhancement to HTML that helps make non-semantic elements more accessible. In this lesson, you explored how meaningful structure, using elements like headings, lists, landmarks, and roles, supports a more navigable and understandable experience for users of assistive technologies. As you build and review your own content, consider how your page structure guides users. Are you relying on ARIA where native HTML would be better, or overlooking places where ARIA could improve clarity and access? Thoughtful use of both structure and ARIA ensures a more inclusive web experience.")
    end
end
